{
  "title": "Con la presente, {ip:desideriamo} informarLa",
  "translation": "We hereby wish to inform you",
  "topics": "Word formation and compounds, register and style, punctuation in long sentences",
  "grammar": {
    "explain": [
      {
        "h": "Compound words"
      },
      "Italian forms compounds by joining two words, and the type of compound decides its plural.",
      {
        "table": {
          "head": [
            "Parts",
            "Example",
            "Plural"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "verb + noun",
              "il portafoglio, l'asciugamano",
              "i portafogli, gli asciugamani"
            ],
            [
              "noun + noun",
              "il capolavoro, l'arcobaleno",
              "i capolavori, gli arcobaleni"
            ],
            [
              "noun + adjective",
              "la cassaforte",
              "le casseforti"
            ],
            [
              "capo + person",
              "il capostazione",
              "i capistazione"
            ]
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "h": "The feeling behind a suffix"
      },
      "The same suffix can praise or belittle, depending on the context. *Un lavoretto* may be a small job or a pat on the back, and *un ministrucolo* is clearly contemptuous. *-astro* (*poetastro*) and *-ucolo* always carry a negative judgement. A suffix can also make a new word: *cavallo → cavalletto* (an easel).",
      {
        "h": "Nouns from verbs"
      },
      "Formal writing replaces verbs with nouns. This is called nominal style: *Dopo che il contratto {pp:è stato} {pp:firmato}* becomes *Dopo la firma del contratto*. Use it moderately, as too much of it makes a text heavy.",
      {
        "h": "Four registers, one idea"
      },
      {
        "table": {
          "head": [
            "Register",
            "Example"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "Conversation",
              "Se non {ip:paghi}, {ip:sono} guai."
            ],
            [
              "Journalism",
              "In caso di mancato pagamento, {ip:sono previste} sanzioni."
            ],
            [
              "Academic",
              "Il mancato pagamento {cop:comporterebbe} l'applicazione di sanzioni."
            ],
            [
              "Bureaucratic",
              "Il mancato versamento {fs:darà} luogo all'irrogazione delle sanzioni previste."
            ]
          ]
        }
      },
      "Formal texts prefer verbs like *recarsi* (*andare*), *effettuare* (*fare*), *comunicare* (*dire*) and *ritenere* (*pensare*). Formulas include *Con la presente* (Hereby), *In riferimento a* (With reference to) and *Restando in attesa* (Looking forward to).",
      "In a very formal letter, the courtesy pronouns are capitalised, even when attached: *informarLa, inviarLe*.",
      {
        "h": "Punctuation in long sentences"
      },
      {
        "table": {
          "head": [
            "Case",
            "Example"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "A comma before and after an insertion",
              "Il direttore, a quanto {ip:pare}, si {pp:è dimesso}."
            ],
            [
              "Both commas or none",
              "Il sindaco, che {im:era} assente, {pp:ha scritto}."
            ],
            [
              "A semicolon between long items",
              "{ip:Sono} presenti: Rossi, sindaco; Bianchi, assessore."
            ],
            [
              "Dashes for a stronger break",
              "La decisione – presa ieri – {ip:è} definitiva."
            ],
            [
              "No comma before che in a restrictive clause",
              "Il libro che {pp:ho letto} {ip:è} bello."
            ]
          ]
        }
      },
      "A subordinate clause placed before the main clause is followed by a comma: *Sebbene {csi:fosse} tardi, {pr:continuò}*. After the main clause, a comma depends on how closely the two parts are linked."
    ]
  },
